  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe are to find a improved upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem constant C, which is the minimum C such that there exists a step function h:[0,2]->[0,1] with integral 1 that satisfies max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx \u2264 C.\n\nThe central idea is to use a reformulation of the optimization problem to leverage the smooth variation of the correlation function. We note that for step functions with many pieces, the maximum correlation often occurs at small lags, which can be captured by considering a discrete set of lags. This suggests that we can approximate the continuous maximum by a finite sum, allowing us to use a gradient-based optimization.\n\nThe approach involves:\n- Considering a discrete set of lags from -n+1 to n-1\n- Using exponential moving average to smooth the step function candidate\n- Optimizing a truncated version of the objective function\n- Scaling the number of pieces based on time available\n\nThe primary limitation is that finite lag set approximation may not capture all extrema, but it has proven effective for similar piecewise constant optimization problems.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nVariables:\n- n: number of samples (100 to begin, scaled if time allows)\n- h: piecewise constant function values (normalization condition)\n\nObjective:\nMinimize max_{k=-(n-1)}^{n-1} w_k * (1/n) * |sum_{i} h[i] * (1 - h[(i+k+n-1)%(2*n-1)])| \n\nwhere weights w_k are chosen to favor small k (as they might be more significant in practice).\n\nConstraints:\n0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n(2*n-1)/n * sum(h) = 1   (integral normalization)\n\nThe correlation for lag k is approximated as:\nC_k = (dx/n) * sum_{i} h[i] * (1 - h[(i+k)%(2*n-1)])\n\nWith dx = 2*(n-1)/(2*n-1)\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nParent Validation:\n- Verify n_points is multiple of 50 for interpolation\n- Ensure sum(initial_h_values) \u2248 n_points/2 within tolerance\n\nInitialization:\n- Start with piecewise constant interpolant at current n\n- Use uniform random sampling with sum adjusted\n\nCore Optimization:\n1. Use gradient descent on the estimated max correlation\n2. Start from projected feasible point\n3. Maintain adaptive step size decay\n4. Use exponential moving average to smooth step function\n5. Cap the number of points based on time budget\n6. If plateaus, try denser grid (increase n by 2x)\n\nGradient Calculation:\n- For each lag, compute correlation's vector field\n- Set gradient proportional to signed correlation difference\n- Clipping and projection to maintain feasibility\n\nUpdate Schedule:\n- Temperature linear decay until improvement plateaus\n- Use 80% decay rate on projections\n- Maximum 600 iterations (unless time exhausted)\n\nTermination Conditions:\n- Stagnation exceeding 800 linear combinations\n- Time budget belief\n- Entropy condition (using exponential families)\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Lag set: fixed sequence from -(n-1) to (n-1) step 5\n- Projection: iterative mixing (priority: integral, bounds)\n- Evolution: (5\u00d74+2) dimensional optimization (15 variables)\n- Variable Generation: piecewise (avoid fine-grained moves)\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Target 3\u00d710\u2076 operations for 1100 seconds\n- Primary candidate on 50-100 points (cost 10\u2076 operations)\n- When time tight: binary search for best n in 50,70,90,110,...\n- Fallback to borrowed artifacts (if time exceeds 3 seconds)\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Post-normalization: check sum exactly n/2 (using exponential averaging)\n- Feasibility: min h=0, max h=1\n- Score: computed via full correlation using n_points+3\n- Fallible Template: disjoint interpolation if parent unavailable\n\nFor each step, we maintain the mathematical properties and optimization path.",
